7592186483600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484177 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484177 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484177 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484177 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484177 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484177 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484177 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186484016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484177 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cab925e1-8606-5f87-ab70-d0c31c22a818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484198 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484198 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484198 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484198 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ be square matrices. If $A=B\\cdot C,$  $|B|=24$ and $|{C}^{-1}|=4$, find $|A|.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484198 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "099dc702-c2e9-5790-a5a1-762c52cdef4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484198 #edb/ref 1074 "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nAlso, given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $M,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({M}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(M)}.\n$$\n\nSince $|C|=\\frac{1}{|{C}^{-1}|}$, we get $|C|=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|B|\\cdot |C| \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484199 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484199 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484199 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484199 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484199 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bcf73b5-94e8-5857-a9f9-54d536a26fba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484199 #edb/ref 1074 "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(8-(-3))\\cdot (-5-1) \\\\  & =11\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484211 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484211 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484211 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484211 #edb/ref 1071 "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 205 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{aligned}|.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484211 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c0dc310-8159-56fb-918f-2199e65fa375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484211 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 205 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202+0 & 203+0 & 204+1 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202+0 & 203+0 & 204+1 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 202 & 203\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4\\cdot 203-6\\cdot 202) \\\\  & =-(812-1\\,212) \\\\  & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484234 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484234 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484234 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484234 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $H=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(H).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484234 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76f24dd6-dfcb-56d2-ac88-5f5a55e3e851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484234 #edb/ref 1074 "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(H)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\cdot 3(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(2-(-6)\\cdot 3(2-3) \\\\  & =32\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484286 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484286 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42580" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484286 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484286 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a & b-1\\end{aligned}|=5$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & b\\end{aligned}|.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484286 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb18f7db-5036-553a-815a-b1774de95355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484286 #edb/ref 1074 "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & b\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & (b-1)+1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & (b-1)+1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a & b-1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{5}+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+(2-8) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
